/* Stylesheet for the Qt UI plugin (src/ui/qt/), loaded at runtime by
   MainWindow. Edit this file and restart the app to see changes; nothing
   here needs a rebuild.

   Colors are taken from tdesktop's own "day-blue" theme (Telegram Desktop's
   default light theme), extracted from telegram_fork/tdesktop/Telegram/
   Resources/day-blue.tdesktop-theme (a zip archive; unzip and read
   colors.tdesktop-theme to see the source values) - not guessed, so the
   look matches the real client rather than an approximation of it. */

QMainWindow, QWidget#central {
    background-color: #e6ebee;
}

/* Chat wallpaper is set in C++ (see MainWindow's chatWallpaperTile(),
   main_window.cpp), not here - real Telegram Desktop composites a tiled
   pattern mask over a gradient with a blend mode (see
   data/data_wall_paper.cpp in telegram_fork/tdesktop), which plain QSS
   background-image cannot do (no blend modes, no separate opacity per
   layer). #messages (HistoryCanvas, see main_window.cpp) intentionally
   has no background rule at all here; do not add one back, it would just
   paint over the composited tile applied to its viewport in code. */

QWidget#header {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #e4e4e5;
}

/* Draggable divider between the sidebar and the conversation, see
   MainWindow::MainWindow's QSplitter - thin and the same color as the
   sidebar's own border it replaces, only a faint highlight on hover to
   signal it can be dragged. */

QSplitter#mainSplitter::handle {
    background-color: #e4e4e5;
    width: 1px;
}

QSplitter#mainSplitter::handle:hover {
    background-color: #b9c1c6;
}

/* Sidebar (chat list), see MainWindow::MainWindow - list of every Telegram
   chat from TDLib, not just conversations with an active encrypted
   session. #chatListWidget (SidebarCanvas, see main_window.cpp) draws
   every row itself, virtualized, not built from real per-row widgets -
   see renderSidebarRows(). */

QWidget#sidebar {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    border-right: 1px solid #e4e4e5;
}

#chatListWidget {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    border: none;
    outline: none;
}

/* Sidebar rows are custom-painted, not QLabels on a WA_StyledBackground
   QWidget (see SidebarRowWidget in main_window.cpp) - background,
   hover/selected state, name/preview text, and the unread badge are all
   drawn directly there, not styled here. */

/* The write-restriction bar (see writeRestrictionBar_ in main_window.cpp)
   - one flat bar the same background as the composer below it
   (historyComposeAreaBg, white - see #composeBar's own comment further
   down), a gray label on the left, a small action button on the right,
   matching the structure of tdesktop's own write-restriction bar (see
   Controls::WriteRestriction/ComposeControls::initWriteRestriction() in
   telegram_fork/tdesktop's history_view_compose_controls.cpp) instead of
   the old two separate stacked bars (a big centered button, then a whole
   separate full-width colored warning strip) with large empty gaps
   between them. */
QWidget#writeRestrictionBar {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    border: none;
}

/* companionLabel_/plainSendWarning_/recordTime_ were all tried as
   Ui::FlatLabel (2026-08-17) and all reverted back to QLabel in the same
   pass - one crashed (Ui::Text::BlockParser on real dynamic chat-title
   text), one went invisible (plainSendWarning_ - the private/plain-mode
   indicator disappearing is what the user actually noticed and reported),
   the third (recordTime_) reverted as a precaution rather than waiting for
   a third bug report. Root cause not found yet - see TODO.md before
   trying any of these three again.
   statusLabel_ is still a plain QLabel but is never actually shown (see its
   own comment in main_window.cpp - setConnectionStatus() drives auth-screen
   switching but the pill itself is not added to the header), so converting
   its styling was not worth new .style declarations for something with no
   visible effect. */

QLabel#companionLabel {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: #222222;
}

QLabel#plainSendWarning {
    color: #999999;
    font-size: 13px;
}

QLabel#recordTime {
    color: #222222;
    font-family: "DejaVu Sans Mono", "Consolas", monospace;
    font-size: 14px;
}

/* chatSearchInput_/searchInput_ are real Ui::InputField now (st::dialogsFilter,
   see main_window.cpp) - paint themselves from their own .style declaration,
   ignoring this stylesheet entirely, same reasoning as the composer field
   comment below. No more QLineEdit anywhere in this codebase. */

/* The composer bar itself (see #composeBar in main_window.cpp) -
   historyComposeAreaBg background (white, #ffffff), no rounding
   (radius: 0px in chat_helpers.style's ComposeControls struct) - the
   same white as the field inside it (historyComposeField.textBg is the
   very same historyComposeAreaBg token), so the field reads as flush
   with the bar instead of a separate rounded box floating in a
   differently-colored row. */
QWidget#composeBar {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    border: none;
}

QWidget#recordBar {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    border: none;
}

/* recordTime_ is a real Ui::FlatLabel now (st::zkgramRecordTime) - paints
   itself. Dropped the monospace font-family override in the move (real
   .style font() declarations in this vendored subset take a size/weight,
   not an arbitrary family list) - a cosmetic loss (digits no longer
   perfectly aligned as they change), not a functional one. */

/* ChatInput (composeField) is a real Ui::InputField now (see main_window.hpp),
   and the attach/send-or-mic buttons are real Ui::IconButton (zkgramAttachButton/
   zkgramSendButton in zkgram_icons.style) - both paint themselves, ignoring
   this stylesheet entirely, same reasoning as the bubble comment below. */

/* Outgoing/incoming/photo message bubbles are custom-painted, not
   QLabels (see HistoryCanvas in main_window.cpp) - color, corner radius,
   and the message tail are all drawn directly in code there, a plain QSS
   border-radius rule cannot produce a bubble with a pointed tail on one
   corner. Only the plain system status line below is still a real
   QLabel. */
QLabel[messageKind="system"] {
    color: #8a8a8a;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 13px;
}

/* Full-window onboarding slide (AuthSlide, see main_window.cpp/.hpp) -
   local password, phone number, login code, 2FA password all reuse this
   one slide. Its background is painted directly in C++
   (AuthSlide::paintEvent(), a flat st::windowBg fill matching real
   Telegram's own login screen exactly, per explicit user request - the
   rest of the app keeps zkgram's purple identity, this screen does not) -
   no QSS rule needed here, any background-color set on #authSlide would
   just be painted over and never seen. */

/* title_/subtitle_/error_ are real Ui::FlatLabel (zkgramAuthLabel style),
   plainInput_/passwordInput_ are real Ui::MaskedInputField/Ui::PasswordInput,
   next_ is a real Ui::RoundButton (defaultActiveButton) - all paint
   themselves from their own .style declarations, ignoring this stylesheet
   entirely (see zkgram_icons.style, zkgram_chat_style_stub.style). */
